course: dictionary
lesson: 7267
title: Pill: a medicine to swallow
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A pill is a small, round or flat piece of medicine that you swallow whole. The words around it give clues: «Never joke about swallowing pills or being hurt; if it is true, tell an adult at once.» Swallowing and hurt tell you that a pill goes into your body and can be dangerous if it is not taken properly.
explain: Pills can look like sweets, so you must never take one unless a grown-up you trust has given it to you. «A pill box with the days of the week helps someone see whether today's tablets have been taken and avoid a double dose.» A double dose means taking too much medicine by mistake. Always ask: did a parent, doctor or pharmacist give me this, and is it the right amount?
explain: Pill also appears in the phrase «a bitter pill to swallow». Here nobody is swallowing medicine. The meaning is bad news that you have to accept, even though you do not like it. Use the rest of the sentence to check: if the news is unpleasant and you must accept it, the phrase fits.
fact: A pill is a small, round or flat piece of medicine that you swallow whole.
fact: «Pills» can look like sweets, so only take them when a parent, doctor or pharmacist gives them to you.
fact: Take only the right dose, which means the right amount at the right time.
fact: «A bitter pill to swallow» means bad news that you have to accept.
fact: «Before a new medicine is sold, it is tested on many volunteers and compared with a dummy pill» to make sure it really works and is safe.
exercise: «Never joke about swallowing pills or being hurt.» What is a pill? | A sweet that you suck slowly | *A small, round or flat piece of medicine that you swallow whole | A bowl used for soup | A tool for cutting paper
exercise: «A pill box with the days of the week helps someone avoid a double dose.» What does double dose mean? | *Taking too much medicine by mistake | Getting two sweets from the shop | Having two baths in one day | Taking medicine on a Sunday only
exercise: Who should give you a pill? | Your best friend at school | *A parent, doctor or pharmacist | A stranger in the park | A pill you found on the ground
test: «Before a new medicine is sold, it is tested on many volunteers and compared with a dummy pill.» What is a dummy pill used for? | A pill that costs a lot of money | A pill for dogs only | *A harmless fake pill, used to check that the real medicine works | A sweet to eat at lunch
test: «A multivitamin can't make up for a poor diet, because food gives fibre, energy and many substances a pill does not contain.» What does this tell you? | Food is never useful |  | A pill can replace healthy food | Pills always contain more fibre than food | *A pill cannot replace healthy food
exam: Look at «a bitter pill to swallow». What does it mean? | A very big pill that is hard to swallow | A pill you must eat with cake | *Bad news that you have to accept | A medicine that tastes very nice
